<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso III of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1265">1265</a>–<a href="/years/1291">1291</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso the Liberal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso III of Aragon (1265–1291), called the Liberal, was King of Aragon and Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1285. Facing the noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-aragon">Union of Aragon</a>, he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilege-of-the-union">Privilege of the Union</a> in 1287, which greatly limited royal power. In the same year he conquered Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>, enslaving or expelling much of its population. He died in 1291 and was succeeded by his brother James II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Alfonso III of Aragon" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonym - vers 1315-1325 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfons_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…France and the papacy over Sicily, nobles and towns organised in the Union of Aragon forced him to grant the General Privilege, and in 1287 <b>Alfonso III</b> yielded the still wider Privileges of the Union, until Peter IV crushed the Union at the Battle of Épila in 1348.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1287 <b>Alfonso III of Aragon</b> invaded, accusing the island of aiding the Hafsid Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-menorca">Taifa of Menorca</a></li></ul></section>
